{
 "comment": "Horde composition and scaling. A round picks mobs whose [minRound,maxRound] window contains it, weighted. Boss rounds come from bosses.json instead. Behaviours: slime-hops, walker, flyer, caster. The shop's tier gate opens a tier every six rounds: at three the whole 140-weapon roster was purchasable by round 15, which is the first quarter of a boss cycle, and every shop after that was a reroll of things you could already buy. At six the roster finishes opening around round 27 and boss bags carry the early power spikes instead. The offset is a head start: without it the first five rounds share four tier-one weapons and the shop has nothing to say, so tier two still lands on round three and only the later steps slow down. Enemy health takes whichever is larger of the linear ramp and a compounding one. Player power compounds -- tier, reforges, ammo and potions all multiply -- while a linear wave grew 7.5x over 55 rounds against a 180x rise in damage, so late hordes cleared in about a second. The compounding rate is set to sit under the linear ramp until round 20 and overtake it after, so nothing before that changes and the endgame stays a fight. Contact damage and coin value stay linear on purpose: bosses already kill in three hits, and coins are already in surplus.",
